1. e4 { White occupies e4, gaining central space and opening lines for the bishop and queen. } 1... e5 { Black occupies e5 and contests the centre with a pawn of its own. } 2. Nf3 { White develops the knight to f3 and attacks the pawn on e5. } 2... Nc6 { Black develops the knight to c6 and protects e5. } 3. Bc4 { White develops the bishop to c4, where it looks along the diagonal toward f7. This does not by itself create a forced attack. } 3... Bc5 { Black develops the bishop to c5, bringing a piece into play on an active diagonal. } 4. d3 { White supports e4 with d3 and opens the diagonal for the bishop on c1. } 4... Nf6 { Black develops the knight to f6 and puts pressure on e4. } 5. O-O { White castles kingside, moving the king away from the centre and bringing the rook closer to the central files. } 5... d6 { Black supports e5 with d6 and opens the other bishop's diagonal. } 6. c3 { White plays c3 to support a possible d4 advance. The pawn move also changes the squares available to the queenside knight. } 6... O-O { Black castles kingside. Both kings are now sheltered while the central pawns remain in place. } 7. Re1 { White brings the rook to e1 to support e4 and prepare central play. } 7... a6 { Black advances the a-pawn, gaining a little space and making a7 available as a retreat square for the bishop. } 8. Bb3 { White moves the bishop to b3, keeping its diagonal toward the centre and Black's kingside. } 8... Ba7 { Black retreats the bishop to a7, retaining the diagonal toward f2. } 9. Nbd2 { White develops the queenside knight to d2, where it supports e4 and can be rerouted. } 9... h6 { Black plays h6, controlling g5 and giving the king a possible escape square later. } 10. Nf1 { White reroutes the knight to f1. From there it can go toward g3 or e3 after checking the position. } 10... Be6 { Black develops the remaining bishop to e6. This is a useful stopping point for the lesson: both sides now choose a middlegame plan, rather than follow a single forced continuation. } *

