[Event "Scotch Game - exchange and develop · White"]
[Site "Chess for Anki"]
[Date "2026.11.16"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Learner"]
[Black "Saved line"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 { White occupies e4 and opens lines for the bishop and queen. } 1... e5 { Black meets White in the centre with e5. } 2. Nf3 { White develops the knight to f3 and attacks e5. } 2... Nc6 { Black develops the knight to c6 and defends the central pawn. } 3. d4 { White challenges the centre immediately with d4. This starts the Scotch Game. } 3... exd4 { Black exchanges the e-pawn for White's pawn on d4. } 4. Nxd4 { White recaptures with the knight and places it in the centre. } 4... Bc5 { Black develops the bishop to c5, putting pressure on the knight on d4. } 5. Be3 { White develops the bishop to e3 and gives the knight additional support. } 5... Qf6 { Black brings the queen to f6, adding pressure along the f-file and toward the centre. } 6. c3 { White supports d4 with c3 and gives the central knight a more secure base. } 6... Nge7 { Black develops the knight to e7, keeping the f-pawn free and preparing to castle. } 7. Bc4 { White develops the bishop to c4, pointing toward the kingside. } 7... Ne5 { Black brings the queenside knight to e5, gaining central activity and attacking the bishop on c4. } 8. Be2 { White retreats the bishop to e2, keeping it safe and clearing the way to castle. } 8... d6 { Black plays d6 to support the centre and open the diagonal for the remaining bishop. } 9. O-O { White castles kingside and brings the rook closer to the central files. } 9... O-O { Black castles kingside as well. Each side now has a safer king before further central play. } 10. Nd2 { White develops the last knight to d2. With the kings castled, the next lesson is to compare central breaks and improve the remaining pieces rather than memorize a supposed final opening move. } *

