[Result "*"]

1. d4 { White occupies d4 and takes central space. } 1... d5 { Black occupies d5 and contests the centre. } 2. Nf3 { White develops the kingside knight, supporting d4 and preparing to castle. } 2... Nf6 { Black develops the kingside knight to f6 and supports d5. } 3. Bf4 { White develops the bishop to f4 before placing the e-pawn on e3. } 3... e6 { Black supports d5 with e6 and opens the diagonal for the bishop on f8. } 4. e3 { White supports d4 with e3 and opens the light-squared bishop's diagonal. } 4... c5 { Black challenges d4 with c5. A familiar setup still requires attention to the opponent's central play. } 5. c3 { White adds support to d4 with c3, building a compact central structure. } 5... Nc6 { Black develops the queenside knight to c6 and increases pressure on the centre. } 6. Nbd2 { White develops the other knight to d2. It helps control e4 and leaves the c-pawn in place. } 6... Bd6 { Black develops the bishop to d6, offering an exchange of the active bishop on f4. } 7. Bg3 { White keeps that bishop by retreating to g3. } 7... O-O { Black castles kingside and moves the king away from the central tension. } 8. Bd3 { White develops the bishop to d3 and points it toward Black's kingside. } 8... b6 { Black prepares a home for the remaining bishop on b7. } 9. Qe2 { White brings the queen to e2, adding support to e4 and preparing to connect the rooks by castling. } 9... Bb7 { Black develops the bishop to b7, where it looks toward the centre. } 10. O-O { White castles kingside. The basic setup is in place; deciding whether to prepare e4 or improve a piece now depends on the position. } *

