[Black "Saved line"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 { White occupies e4, taking central space and opening lines for the pieces. } 1... c5 { Black plays c5, controlling d4 from the flank and entering the Sicilian Defence. } 2. Nf3 { White develops the knight to f3 and prepares a central d4 advance. } 2... d6 { Black supports the central squares with d6 and opens the bishop's diagonal. } 3. d4 { White challenges the centre with d4. } 3... cxd4 { Black exchanges the c-pawn for the pawn on d4, opening the c-file. } 4. Nxd4 { White recaptures with the knight, placing it actively in the centre. } 4... Nf6 { Black develops the knight to f6 and attacks e4. } 5. Nc3 { White develops the knight to c3 and defends e4. } 5... Nc6 { Black develops the queenside knight to c6 and increases central influence. } 6. Be2 { White develops the bishop to e2 and prepares to castle. } 6... e6 { Black supports d5 with e6 and opens the diagonal for the bishop on f8. } 7. O-O { White castles kingside, sheltering the king before further central action. } 7... Be7 { Black develops the bishop to e7 and prepares to castle too. } 8. Be3 { White develops the bishop to e3, supporting the centre and coordinating the pieces. } 8... O-O { Black castles kingside. Both sides have brought the king to safety. } 9. f4 { White advances the f-pawn to gain space and support e5. Pawn advances also leave squares behind, so further play must be calculated. } 9... a6 { Black controls b5 with a6 and prepares possible queenside expansion. } 10. Kh1 { White moves the king to h1, leaving the diagonal toward b6 and making room for future rook coordination. } 10... Qc7 { Black places the queen on c7, where it supports central and queenside play. } 11. Qe1 { White moves the queen to e1, connecting the pieces and keeping several plans available. This is a teaching transition to the middlegame, not a claim that the opening has one exact finishing point. } *

